  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1043 PM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nWarm and quiet weather will continue on Monday. Mostly sunny \nconditions are expected with the exception of a few high\nclouds in the mid to late afternoon for the northern plains and \nhigher terrain. Highs will be in the mid 60s to around 70 degrees,\nwhich is 10 to 15 degrees above normal for this time of year. \n\nModels are in better agreement on the positioning of the upper level \nshortwave Tuesday into Wednesday. The GFS has trended more \nnortheast, with the other models trending more southeast, meeting in \nthe middle of what they've been showing the past couple of days. \nAlthough there will be some lift associated with the shortwave, \nmoisture is too low when it moves through for precipitation \ndevelopment. At the surface, a cold front will move through mid \nTuesday into early Wednesday. Winds behind the front will be breezy \nfrom the west/northwest which will allow for decent downsloping and \nwarm temperatures Tuesday afternoon. This could lead to elevated \nfire weather conditions across portions of the northern plains \nTuesday afternoon. Cooler (but still above-normal temperatures) will \nexist for Wednesday. However, these slightly cooler temperatures\nwill be short- live as our next upper level ridge starts to build\nbehind the shortwave.\n\nUpper level ridging will develop over the western U.S. Wednesday \nand Thursday. An upper level low will approach the southwest coast\nof California Friday and Saturday, pushing the ridge axis over \nthe area. Temperatures will remain above normal and dry Thursday \nthrough the weekend, with our next hope for precipitation early \nnext week as the upper highs gets ushered out of our area.",
